{
  "dom_id": "step-q120176",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/440d321dd0eb1b5687b392aac95d5d7d36e5a31d4cb95d5397f88afe229d7aa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/0a8b34730f001c218b6c380b9f5af0ff4b2ffd8fbb295f82e4961c5242051986.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={y}^{2}-2y=y(y-2)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\u2197$ | $\u2197$ | $\u2198$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{\u2032}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}$",
